.TH KEYCHARTEST 1
.SH NAME
keychartest \- check that a key event's characters arrive whole
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B keychartest
.I string
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Keychartest
reads
.B /dev/keyboard
and compares what arrives against
.IR string ,
rune for rune.
.PP
It is meant to be run with
.BR INFERNO_KEY_TEST ,
which makes
.IR emu (1)
push a string through the same path a key event takes:
.IP
.EX
s='h\e[u00e9]llo'
INFERNO_KEY_TEST=$s emu-cocoa /dis/keychartest.dis $s
.EE
.PP
The interesting characters are the ones above the basic multilingual
plane.
.B NSString
is UTF-16, so an emoji is a surrogate
.IR pair ,
and code that takes one unit of it delivers half a rune \(em a lone high
surrogate, which is not a character.
Reported as a count of both, so the difference is visible:
.B "héllo 😀 世界"
is 11 UTF-16 units and 10 runes.
.SS Why the hook exists
Nothing else in the system can produce a surrogate pair on demand.
A person has to type one, and writing to
.B /dev/keyboard
goes in
.I below
the platform, so it exercises none of the decoding.
That leaves a fault in the one path a person's keyboard actually takes
untestable, which is what
.B INFERNO_KEY_TEST
is for \(em the same shape and the same reason as
.B INFERNO_RESIZE_TEST
in
.IR emu (1).
.SS The watchdog
A read of
.B /dev/keyboard
blocks, so a run that delivers fewer runes than were asked for would wait
for ever, and a test that hangs reports nothing at all \(em which is
exactly what an earlier version of this did when the delivery was broken.
A second process writes one
.B EOT
after four seconds to end the wait.
It writes into the same queue the platform feeds, below the code being
tested, so it says nothing about that code: it is a timer, not a probe.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/keychartest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR emu (1),
.IR keyuptest (1),
.IR cons (3)
.SH BUGS
It checks that characters arrive, not that composition works.
There is no
.B NSTextInputClient
on the view, so an input method still has nowhere to show a composition
while it is being made; what this proves is only that a finished string
survives the crossing.
.PP
The hook delivers with a keyCode of zero, so it exercises the text path
and never the one that turns a keyCode into Return or an arrow.
.PP
Cocoa only. Every other backend has its own keyboard path and none of
them is checked here.
